      "id": "q_00031",
      "category": "direct",
      "question": "In postgresql, what are the key requirements and operational mechanics for postgresql?",
      "answer": "**Key requirements**\n\n- Store data in relational tables with rows, typed columns, primary keys and foreign‑key relationships; normalize data but allow deliberate denormalization when read‑performance or analytics demand it. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html]  \n- Back‑up **all** stateful components – PostgreSQL data files, persistent volumes and application configuration – and define clear recovery objectives that are regularly tested. [Source: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/]  \n- Use PostgreSQL as the authoritative store for structured metadata and operational state; do **not** rely on it as a substitute for a vector‑search index unless the architecture is intentionally built that way. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html]  \n- Choose a transaction isolation level that meets correctness needs while balancing concurrency; the weakest acceptable level should be used rather than assuming the strictest level is always best. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html]  \n\n**Operational mechanics**\n\n- **MVCC**: PostgreSQL implements Multi‑Version Concurrency Control using `xmin`/`xmax` transaction visibility metadata. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/postgres_mvcc_isolation_vacuum.html]  \n- **VACUUM & Autovacuum**:  \n  - `VACUUM` reclaims dead tuples and prevents table bloat.  \n  - Autovacuum runs automatically; a typical tuning value is `autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.05` to avoid bloat and XID wrap‑around. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/postgres_mvcc_isolation_vacuum.html]  \n- **Configuration defaults for performance** (example from an enterprise cluster):  \n  - `shared_buffers = 1024MB`  \n  - `work_mem = 32MB`  \n  - `checkpoint_completion_target = 0.9`  \n  These settings help optimise query latency and write throughput. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/postgres_mvcc_isolation_vacuum.html]  \n- **Isolation levels**: PostgreSQL supports multiple isolation levels (e.g., READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE) that trade off concurrency against anomaly prevention; the application should select the level that satisfies its correctness requirements. [Source: https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html]  \n\nTogether, these requirements and mechanics define how PostgreSQL should be deployed, backed up, tuned, and used within a production environment.",
